.TH LOGSCRUB 1 "October 2026" "logscrub" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
logscrub \- mask secrets and personal data in logs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B logscrub
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR ...]
.br
.B logscrub serve
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B logscrub completion
bash|zsh|fish
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B logscrub
reads the given files (or standard input when there are none, or for \fB-\fR) and writes the text with secrets and personal data replaced by markers such as \fB[REDACTED:email]\fR, so a log can be pasted into a bug report, a ticket or a chat.
.PP
Matching uses linear-time (RE2) patterns, so crafted input cannot cause catastrophic backtracking. Memory is bounded by the longest line; a line that cannot be scanned safely is an error, never passed through. logscrub makes no network connections, and its errors and reports never contain the text it found.
.PP
Pattern matching cannot find every secret. Review the output before sharing it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Write nothing; exit with status 1 if anything would be masked. For use in CI.
.TP
.B \-\-report
Print counts per detector, never the matched text. To standard error, or to standard output with \fB\-\-check\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-report\-format " FORMAT"
Format of \fB\-\-report\fR: \fBtext\fR (default), \fBjson\fR, or \fBsarif\fR (SARIF 2.1.0, as understood by GitHub code scanning). JSON and SARIF list where each match is (file, line, byte offset) but never what it is.
.TP
.BI \-\-enable " IDS"
Comma-separated detector ids to add to the defaults (for the opt-in detectors).
.TP
.BI \-\-disable " IDS"
Comma-separated detector ids to turn off.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-detectors
List the detector ids with a description and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " MODE"
\fBmarker\fR (default) replaces matches with \fB[REDACTED:id]\fR. \fBpseudonym\fR replaces emails, IP addresses, IBANs, user names in home paths and MAC addresses with stable tags such as \fB[email:k3x9p2qa]\fR derived from an HMAC key, so the same value always gets the same tag. Secrets always use markers.
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-file " FILE"
File holding the pseudonym HMAC key (at least 16 bytes), or set \fBLOGSCRUB_KEY\fR. The key is never taken from the command line. Without one a random key is used and tags are stable within the run only.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
TOML rules file: \fBenable\fR and \fBdisable\fR lists, an allow-list (\fB[[allow]]\fR with \fBexact\fR and \fBregex\fR), and custom RE2 rules (\fB[[rule]]\fR). Parsed strictly.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Do not read the per-user config file.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIFILE\fR"
Write the cleaned text to \fIFILE\fR instead of standard output. The file is written atomically with mode 0600, and logscrub refuses to overwrite a file it is reading. Cannot be combined with \fB\-\-check\fR. If \fIFILE\fR ends in \fB.gz\fR the output is gzip-compressed.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-in\-place
Rewrite each \fIfile\fR with its cleaned text instead of writing to standard output. Each file is replaced atomically (temporary file in the same directory, then rename) and keeps its permissions; a gzip file stays gzip. A file with nothing to mask is not touched. A file that cannot be scanned is reported and left as it was, the others are still processed, and the exit status is 3. Needs file arguments; cannot be combined with \fB\-\-check\fR or \fB\-\-output\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-backup\-suffix " SUFFIX"
With \fB\-\-in\-place\fR, keep the original of each changed file as \fIfile\fRSUFFIX (a hard link, so it never overwrites an existing backup; the run fails for that file instead). The backup still holds the secrets. \fB\-\-recursive\fR skips files that end in SUFFIX.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-recursive
Descend into the directories given as arguments. Symbolic links, \fB.git\fR, \fB.hg\fR and \fB.svn\fR directories and binary files (a NUL byte in the first 8000 bytes) are skipped; a count of skipped binary files is printed. Files named on the command line are always processed.
.TP
.BI \-\-include " GLOBS"
With \fB\-\-recursive\fR, comma-separated file name globs; only matching files are processed (default: all).
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " GLOBS"
With \fB\-\-recursive\fR, comma-separated file or directory name globs to skip.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-line\-bytes " N"
Longest line scanned in one piece (default 1048576). Longer lines are scanned in overlapping windows.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-private\-key\-lines " N"
Longest private key block masked line by line (default 200). A longer block is masked up to the limit and a warning is printed.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show the usage and exit.
.SH SERVE
.B logscrub serve
starts a local web UI (default http://127.0.0.1:8080): paste or drop a log, see every match highlighted by detector, toggle detectors, choose markers or stable tags, and download the cleaned text. The text is processed in memory by this process and never stored or logged. The server listens on a loopback address only, checks the Host and Origin of every request, sends a strict Content-Security-Policy and loads nothing from other sites.
.TP
.BI \-\-addr " ADDRESS"
Address to listen on (default 127.0.0.1:8080).
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-remote
Allow a non-loopback address. An access token is then required: unless you supply one (\fB\-\-token\-file\fR or \fBLOGSCRUB_TOKEN\fR), logscrub generates one and prints a URL carrying it in the fragment (\fB#token=...\fR), which browsers never send to a server. The API compares it in constant time. Plain HTTP is not encrypted: use an SSH tunnel or a TLS reverse proxy.
.TP
.BI \-\-token\-file " FILE"
File holding an access token (at least 16 characters) that the API requires as \fBAuthorization: Bearer\fR. Optional on loopback; the page asks for it when needed.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
TOML rules file, as above.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Do not read the per-user config file.
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-file " FILE"
Pseudonym HMAC key file, as above.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-body\-bytes " N"
Largest text accepted (default 10485760).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-line\-bytes " N"
Longest line scanned in one piece.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-private\-key\-lines " N"
Longest private key block masked line by line.
.SH COMPLETION
.B logscrub completion
prints a completion script for bash, zsh or fish, for example \fBlogscrub completion bash > ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/logscrub\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (or, with \fB\-\-check\fR, nothing would be masked).
.TP
.B 1
With \fB\-\-check\fR: something would be masked.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or configuration error.
.TP
.B 3
Read, write or scan error (including a line too long to scan safely).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/logscrub/config.toml
Per-user config, read when it exists and is not world-writable (on Linux \fI~/.config/logscrub/config.toml\fR). logscrub deliberately does not read a config from the current directory: an allow-list in an untrusted checkout could make it leak what it was asked to mask.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LOGSCRUB_KEY
Pseudonym HMAC key, used when \fB\-\-key\-file\fR is not given.
.TP
.B LOGSCRUB_TOKEN
Access token for \fBlogscrub serve\fR, used when \fB\-\-token\-file\fR is not given.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
some-command | logscrub > clean.log
logscrub --check app.log
logscrub --report --report-format sarif --check app.log > logscrub.sarif
logscrub --mode pseudonym --key-file key.txt -o clean.log app.log
logscrub serve
.fi
